# Proves FieldType fires statically for [get k c]: assigning the result of
# [get "name" person] to @Integer produces a type warning because FieldType
# resolves the Indexable constraint to the field's static type, and that type
# cannot unify with Integer.
#
# T-1917: [get k c] carries constraint: [$Indexable c k v] with resolver: FieldType.
# The person dict's "name" field has type StringLiteral("Alice") (from the literal value).
# FieldType(TypeNode.Dict{fields:{name:TypeNode.StringLiteral{s:"Alice"},...}},
#           TypeNode.StringLiteral{s:"name"})
# returns the TypeNode for the "name" field. Since FieldType returns a string-related type
# and Integer is not consistent with any string type, the @Integer annotation on bad
# triggers a unification failure: cannot unify Integer with ...
[
  person: [name: "Alice"  age: 30]
  name@String: [get "name" person]
  bad@Integer: [get "name" person]
]
=== out
Dict({"person": Dict({"name": String("Alice"), "age": Int(30)}), "name": String("Alice"), "bad": String("Alice")})

=== warn
cannot unify Integer with "Alice"
